WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:03.012
(gentle music)

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:07.200
- I chose public health, mainly because.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.290
- I chose public health because.

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:12.360
- My reason for choosing public health

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:16.230
is really deeply rooted in
my personal experiences.

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:18.420
- Many things led me to public health.

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:20.340
- Having worked as a physician in a low

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:21.870
and middle income country,

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:24.510
participating in work in
public health allowed me

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:26.790
to see that we can treat people

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:29.130
or we can address the needs of the people,

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:30.792
not just one patient at a time,

00:00:30.792 --> 00:00:32.430
but in the community as a whole

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:34.830
and that for me was very valuable

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:36.540
'cause working in public health allowed me

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:38.940
to integrate environmental engineering,

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:40.478
medicine and public health

00:00:40.478 --> 00:00:44.100
and see it in a more impactful way.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:46.440
- I remember my family,
we moved from Detroit

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:49.800
to a suburban area outside of the city.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:52.230
And while I was young at the time,

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:54.270
I really couldn't ignore
the stark differences

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:58.260
and racial demographics,
but also urban development

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:00.990
and it really sparked a curiosity,

00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:02.880
didn't know what it looked
like and meant at the time,

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:04.860
and how to really conceptualize that.

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:08.670
Now, understanding that move
that I had when I was younger

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:12.180
from those two desparate
communities, just 30 miles apart,

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:15.437
but had a 10 year difference
in life expectancy,

00:01:15.437 --> 00:01:17.580
that stark difference was something

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:19.020
that propelled me into the field.

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:22.290
- I graduated the year
that Covid-19 pandemic hit.

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:24.660
So who were the frontline
workers during the pandemic?

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:25.830
It was people like my parents.

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:28.350
It was people like those
in my Cuban community,

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:31.650
immigrant community, Latina
community that were still pushed

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:36.240
to or had to go to work and
expose themselves to Covid-19

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:38.880
and I had already seen people's social

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:41.730
and behavioral risk factors
for certain chronic diseases

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:44.940
that just made worse during
the Covid-19 pandemic,

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:47.010
in my own family and in my community

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:50.190
and that's why I came
back to graduate school,

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:51.750
to get my master's in public health

00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:56.070
because I feel like I can
hopefully be a mouthpiece

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:57.450
for my community.

00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:00.720
- I have been doing HIV
projects for almost 10 years,

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:03.780
and my work has always
been community-based.

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:06.870
Just seeing that human
side is so important

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:09.120
in terms of designing projects

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:10.920
that are actually gonna
benefit the populations

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:12.120
we're trying to serve.

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:14.700
Public health isn't just
about health outcomes.

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:17.340
It's about analyzing our society

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:20.400
and the environment that we are a part of

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:23.130
and seeing how that has
an impact on our health.

00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:25.080
- It's one of the fields that I really,

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:29.130
I feel like can alleviate human suffering,

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:31.950
which is something that
I'm passionate about.

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:33.330
- What's really important
about public health

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:35.640
is it's really the bedrock of everything

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:39.810
and it really has components
in almost everything here.

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:41.520
So if we think of all the schools at Yale,

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:44.805
Public Health has a component
within each and every school.

00:02:44.805 --> 00:02:47.638
(inspiring music)

